 Both inputs must be JSON values. Operations are evaluated in order and processing stops at the first
 RFC 6902 error; because the input document is never changed, a failed patch leaves it intact.
+
+## JSON Merge Patch
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies an [RFC 7386 JSON Merge Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386)
+and returns a new JSON document. Object patches recursively merge members; a member whose patch
+value is `null` is removed. Missing members are added, and unmentioned members are preserved.
+Arrays and other non-object patches replace the entire target value, so a root-level `null` patch
+returns `null`.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+applyMergePatch(
+  { name: "Ada", settings: { theme: "light", legacy: true }, tags: ["old"] },
+  { settings: { theme: "dark", legacy: null }, tags: ["new"] },
+);
+// { name: "Ada", settings: { theme: "dark" }, tags: ["new"] }
+```
+
+Both inputs must be JSON values; invalid inputs throw. Neither input is changed, and the result
+shares no mutable containers with either input. Merge Patch is useful for object-shaped partial
+updates; use JSON Patch when you need to update individual array elements or set an object member
+to an explicit `null`.
